2223 0 obj
<<
/Title (The spotted hyena \(Crocuta crocuta\) is a socially complex, ecologically important carnivore whose range-wide genomic variation remains poorly characterised. We analysed 55 individuals from across sub-Saharan Africa using whole-genome and restriction site-associated DNA \(RAD-seq\) data to quantify genetic diversity, infer population structure, and assess gene flow. Heterozygosity ranged from 0.1485 to 0.3688 among populations. Population genomic analyses revealed three major clusters corresponding to western, eastern, and southern Africa, with finer-scale subdivision into five regional groups. D-statistics indicated historical and ongoing gene flow, particularly between eastern and southern populations, while the western cluster was more differentiated and exhibited the lowest genetic diversity. These patterns parallel those observed in other large African carnivores and likely reflect a combination of historical biogeographic processes and recent anthropogenic fragmentation. Our results provide the most geographically comprehensive genomic assessment of spotted hyenas to date and highlight the need for targeted conservation strategies that prioritise restoring connectivity to western populations.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2216 0 R
/Prev 2222 0 R
>>
endobj
