2256 0 obj
<<
/Title (Background: Malaria diseases has become global health challenge due to mosquito resistance to insecticide. Therefore, primary goal of this study was to isolate, characterize and evaluate fungal biocides from pesticide-contaminated environments for their potential use as eco-friendly mosquito control agents. The study insights analyse the metabolic profiles of fungal isolates and the genetic diversity of strains that show more efficiency and the ability to produce potent secondary metabolites. Methodology: Pesticide factory effluent sample was used to isolate and characterized fungal strain using morphological, biochemical and molecular characterizations. The isolates were screened for spore production and larvicidal activities. The spores extracted from the isolates were evaluated using GC-MS. Molecular Ducking was used to identify the interaction of the identified compound with target molecule. Result: A total of thirty fungal isolates exhibiting distinctive morphological and biochemical characteristics were obtained. Among them, two isolates identified as W1 and W4 demonstrated strong potential.These finding showed the potentials of pesticide adapted fungi as a sources of ecofriendly mosquito control with different doses of fungal spore suspension resulted in mosquito larval mortality rate ranging from 6.67% to 100%. Molecular and phylogenetic analysis indicated that isolate W1 showed 99% similarity to the species Penicillium crustosum, while isolate W4 was found to have 100% similarity to the species Rhizopus arrhizus in the NCBI database. The chromatograms analysis of W1 and W4 revealed the presence of 33 and 29 chemical compounds, respectively, while the molecular Docking studies predicted strong binding affinity of several compounds, particularly Phenol, 2-\(1-phenylethyl\) - aligning with recognized insecticide targets. Conclusion: The result of this study suggest that the fungal isolates identified could be a promising candidate as a biocontrol agent for mosquito larvae and adults with optimization of the culture condition and formulation studies.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2243 0 R
/Prev 2250 0 R
/Next 2251 0 R
>>
endobj
